// The loop vectorizer (0.10): an innermost counted loop (induction
// variable i from `start`, step 1, to `n`; scev.vl) whose body is a
// chain of blocks that only branch out to checks that fail (to blocks
// ending in `unreachable`), whose memory accesses are unit-stride loads
// and stores of one scalar type T, and whose only values carried from one
// iteration to the next are i and reductions (`r = r op x` on integers,
// op one of + - * & | ^), gets a vector version: VF = 16 / size(T) lanes
// per iteration, in vector instructions, run for the first
// (n - start) & ~(VF - 1) iterations when it may be, the original loop
// running the rest (the remainder) or all of them.
//
//     P:  jump C                        (the old preheader)
//     C:  trips = n - start; ok = trips >= VF && checks && no overlap
//         branch ok, VP, SP
//     VP: splats, reductions' first vectors (the start value in lane 0,
//         the identity in the others), vend = start + (trips & ~(VF - 1))
//     VH: the body on VF lanes; iv += VF; branch iv != vend, VH, VX
//     VX: reductions summed; branch vend == n, E, SP
//     SP: i and the reductions from C or VX; jump H (the scalar loop)
//
// The vector loop runs only when nothing the scalar loop would check can
// fail in any iteration (each check `idx <u len` with idx = i + k, k
// invariant: idx at start >= 0, idx at n - 1 >= that, and below len) and
// when no store overlaps another access of the loop at a different
// address (ranges over the whole loop, compared at run time); the scalar
// loop runs otherwise, and stops where it would. Results are exactly the
// scalar loop's: lanewise float arithmetic is the same IEEE operations
// (float reductions, which would reassociate, are not vectorized), and
// integer reductions wrap. The scalar loop is marked (BLOCKS[h].novec) so
// that it is not vectorized again.

mod build;
pub mod checks;
mod legality;
mod outer;
pub mod unroll;

